WEBVTT

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:28.360
This is the most determined man I've ever met.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:40.240
The Great Omani.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.920
Inspired by Houdini, he spent a lifetime performing dangerous stunts.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:24.200
And even in the last days of his life, he was still planning his next performance.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.480
I'm still the oldest stuntman in the world.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:49.200
The first time I met the Great Omani, he was being filmed by a South Korean television crew.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:55.920
All I knew about him then was what I had read from local papers as I was growing up in Brighton.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:05.840
That his real name was Ron Cunningham and as a young man, he'd run away from his wealthy family and joined the circus.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.000
A bit more longer please.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.840
Yeah, I tell you when.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.920
So, Ron, um, how is your condition now?

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.160
Well, not too good this morning.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.600
Earlier...

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.720
I had a very bad night.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:32.920
Yesterday, as you may remember, we did about six hours filming and a lot of work went into that and I...

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.760
Sorry, sorry, I'm sorry.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.080
Cut, cut, cut.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:43.760
Because, um, sorry, you know, nobody wants to know that we spent six hours filming.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.120
If we could stick with the story, OK?

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.560
No you can say you were nervous, excited, um yeah?

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.600
No sleep, OK?

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.120
But, um you don't have to say...

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.600
No, I'm sure.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.360
Sorry.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.280
Viewers don't need to know that, I know that.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.640
All right, OK, OK.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:03.560
After the warm up, Ron took the Koreans to the pub next to his house where he wanted to show them what the Great Omani could do.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:19.280
Ladies and gentlemen, the world is full of very weird things and we have one of them here this evening.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.240
This is the Great Omani.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.320
You know, but don't worry about...

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.040
The Great Omaaa-neeee...

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.200
Sorry.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.560
OK.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.920
The world is full of weird and wonderful things.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:44.720
I like to introduce you to one of the most bizarre people I've ever met, the Great Omani.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.320
Oh, my God.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.000
Hey, hup!

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.400
Oh, he's OK.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.880
Oh, I can't believe.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:12.720
I'm going to place the milk bottle on my throat, and smash the bottle with the hammer.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.040
One.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:18.680
Two.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.519
Three.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.680
Oh!

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.840
I'd arranged to meet Ron at his home but then I had an unexpected call from him.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.920
He'd suddenly been taken to hospital and wanted me to come and see him there.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.560
Is Ron Cunningham here?

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.120
Yeah.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.080
Whereabouts is he?

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.960
Ah, he's in bed four.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.520
OK.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.000
Thank you.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.520
Ron.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.040
Oh!

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.600
Sorry.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.640
It's Daniel.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.160
Oh, nice to see you.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.440
Oh, nice to see you.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.160
Sorry to give you a shock.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.400
Were you sleeping?

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:23.840
All I have to do is sit here and dream.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.840
Very nice.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.560
But, um, yeah, a camera.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.400
It's always rolling.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.640
Want sunglasses on or off?

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.200
It's up to you.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.600
They're a bit wonky, aren't they?

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:55.440
Unfortunately, last time we met I was pretty strong and well but I've recently suffered a stroke on the left side which means my left arm is... hardly any strength in it at all.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:01.520
But I think with treatment at this hospital, they will get me well.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:09.640
You see I've still got a good right hand, which is useful for smashing a bottle on the throat and also for picking up the odd pint of beer.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:14.040
That's most important, to keep the right hand going.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:21.120
Actually, at the moment, at this moment of talking, I'm officially the oldest stuntman in the world.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.600
Ron made a quick recovery and was out of the hospital within a week.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.640
The stroke had left him with only partial use of his legs and now he needed full-time help.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:40.400
But Ron did not want to go into a home so he'd persuaded his son David to be his full-time carer.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:48.880
And although David had agreed, it was on one condition - that Ron would finally give up performing dangerous stunts.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.840
Are you OK, David?

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.160
Yeah, of course I'm all right.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.840
It's not all that far now.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.960
Nice coffee?

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.880
Lovely coffee, lovely.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.400
Are you warm enough?

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.960
I'm sweating like a pig.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:19.840
Right, one with me, up.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.600
Same again.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.200
One, two, three, push.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.120
I've got you.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:35.080
Ron and David had lived together since Ron's wife and stage assistant Eileen died 20 years ago.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:44.200
David had reluctantly stepped into the world of show business and taken over his mother's job.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.760
I'm on camera so...

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.320
Well, now, I'm...

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:55.640
I'm hoping to do my last final stunt.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.520
Now I say I'm hoping because...

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.800
David is absolutely against it.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.280
Is that true, David?

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.920
That's perfectly true.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:12.240
Where stunts are concerned, especially now, I'm completely finished.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.000
I never did like show business, personally.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:19.080
He knows that as well as me.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.320
I love show business and he's totally against it.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.120
What are your objections?

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.560
Well, I mean, I just don't want him to do any more.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:31.560
The brain's willing but the body has gone.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:32.200
Simple as that.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:37.520
It's ah, he's a cabbage with a brain still working but the body's not.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:44.240
But this one... if I can get David to see my point of view, which I don't think he will...

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:59.480
Um, I don't want to go against him because he's been so good to me, but there's one big stunt I want to do, one final one, which will be a goodbye stunt, to the newspapers, the people who have been so kind to me, to my public.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:04.920
In fact I could say worldwide because some of my stunts have been filmed worldwide.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.440
But I have got one really good farewell one.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:12.720
I think you'll find David's a very hard, tough man.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.280
David, one moment, please.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:25.840
David is a very tough, very strong, hard man, as you probably may know, but he's also got a heart of gold, heart of gold, and I think you'll find he'll give in, in the end.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.160
I hope.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.680
Anyway, Dave, here's to you and all you've done for me.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:46.240
The next time I saw Ron, he was on his way to hospital for his regular dialysis treatment.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:52.040
His kidneys were failing and he also told me that he'd been diagnosed with prostate cancer.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:10:00.840
Despite this, I knew that Ron's main worry was not his failing health but David's objections to helping him perform another stunt.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.680
What makes him think in that way?

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:10.680
I mean, especially, I would have thought, he'd have been quite proud of it and love it.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:19.800
I mean I've broken quite a number of records and other things and you'd think... as his father, he'd be proud of it but...

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.800
I, I, I just...

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:29.560
I just do not know the answer to that question, why he hates it.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.360
All I can tell you, he definitely does.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:41.640
This is another stunt we've got lined up.

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:59.560
Um, this is a combination of escapology and fire.

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.200
You don't very often see this.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.760
As you see, I'm being securely bound.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.960
Now in a moment there will be a hood placed over my head.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.000
Not just yet I think Dave, otherwise they won't be able to listen to me, nor see me, which is even worse.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.880
It's all in a day's work for me.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.680
But the point is, thing is, one day something is going to happen which he is going to come unstuck.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.600
Well, that's his view.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:25.040
It's in my view, it's been in my mind, been in my mind for donkey's years.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:28.920
Why do you do it, if you're worried he's going to get hurt?

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.640
Well, at least I know what I'm doing.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.680
And someone else...

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.120
Might make a mistake, yes.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.200
Not only make a mistake.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:39.360
With your own father you're just... a little bit more cautious of what you do.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.480
Try and keep your feet down.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.640
That's it.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:06.760
Right.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:10.080
There you are, gentlemen.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.960
I hope you enjoyed it, and, er...

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:19.080
I would think probably, in a few months' time I will have another, shall we say, unusual escape for you.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.120
Do you feel better now?

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:27.840
Yes, dear, thank you.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.000
OK.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:36.440
I said he won't be doing many stunts today.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:52.720
It wasn't long before I had a call from Ron.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:59.520
He'd convinced David to help him perform another stunt by promising that this would be the Great Omani's last-ever show.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:08.280
Hello?

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.160
Hello?

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:10.360
Hello?

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.360
Who is it?

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.480
David, Ron.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:15.760
What?

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:17.240
It's Daniel.

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:17.560
Oh, it's Daniel.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:17.880
Come in Daniel.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:20.800
Come in.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.640
Ah, lovely to see you again.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.480
Well, I'm actually upside down and you can understand...

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:32.800
I had no idea what Ron had planned but I wasn't sure he'd be able to do very much in his condition.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:41.800
My wrists will be chained and padlocked to a steel bar, ah, padlocked, well padlocked up.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:50.520
Then, my coat will be covered with cotton wool which will be petrol-soaked.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:52.360
Then there'll be a chain around the outside of my arms.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:56.640
So, all in all, a pretty tough proposition to get out of.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.400
Now, my assistant will light the petrol and zoom, up it will go.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:09.760
To see a man blazing up on fire, with his arms on fire, his hands chained, padlocked and handcuffed, well, you won't see that very often anywhere.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:14.400
In fact, I don't think you'll see it anywhere at all.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:17.000
I'm a little bit worried about it, I must say.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:20.000
You're a little bit worried about it?

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.480
Well, I must say I am too but... no, I think I've got it pretty well worked out.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:43.720
We're going the main one.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:57.560
Ron had arranged for some journalists to turn up, billing it as the last ever performance of the world's oldest stuntman.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:07.680
Hello, hello.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.000
Right, ready?

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:15.920
Nearly.

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:16.680
What do we put on first?

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.360
The coat?

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:18.440
Well, of course.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:21.520
OK.

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.560
Ah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:22.880
Right.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:43.840
The hat.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:58.960
Then the arms, and then we've only got a few seconds to make my escape in this.

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:21.400
David, put the fire out.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.600
My feet, David.

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:33.600
Ow!

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:36.920
Other side, come on!

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:43.120
Take your coat off.

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:07.359
Ladies and gentlemen.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:13.000
Thank you very much.

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:16.160
You're not going to do it any more.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.960
It proves one thing.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:21.040
I'm getting a bit too old for these stunts.

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:42.760
It got a bit out of control which proves, you see, I've now got to the age, by gosh, I got to this age years ago, where I was thinking about retiring.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:51.760
I was just a bit on the slow side, I definitely got a couple of burns, um, but all in all, I think it went pretty well.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:04.120
I had no idea what was driving Ron to take such risks.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:08.560
It seemed to be something that went a lot deeper than just entertaining and getting his name in the paper.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.760
I wanted to know why he'd chosen this way of life.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:19.560
Ron was not born into a world of show business.

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:25.200
His father, a successful wine importer, had hoped his son would take over the family company.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:27.160
But Ron had other ideas.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:34.840
When his father died, the business collapsed, their manor house was sold and the family fortunes lost.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:19:01.600
Let me tell you something... the Great Omani.

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:03.360
You've got some match on your lip.

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:03.720
Yeah, you're right now.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.120
I'll get you an ashtray, Ron.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:11.120
When I was a child, when I was born, I was born, luckily, to a very wealthy family.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:16.600
I tell you this because I was a very, very backward child.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:20.120
Bottom of every class in every school.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.200
Backward?

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:23.400
I'm surprised.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:23.640
Very, very backward child.

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:27.840
Couldn't read or write for ages.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:38.320
So that makes you wonder why from the most backward boy in any school which would have me, I became the Great Omani.

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:42.600
Did you have something to prove?

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:47.480
A boy did more than any of them.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.160
You've always wanted to be...

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:58.640
Yes.

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:00.520
Yes, I've always wanted to leave my mark.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:01.440
Why?

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.280
For my family's sake as well.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.320
Why did you want to leave your mark?

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:12.160
Well, isn't is rather nice to, to have been someone?

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:20.320
If I leave my mark, it means I've been someone and I think that's rather nice.

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:26.200
Did you want to prove yourself not just to yourself but to your parents as well?

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:28.520
They would have been very proud, wouldn't they, of what you've done?

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.120
My mother would have been.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.480
Yes, I think they would.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:50.160
Beautiful stuff, it's strong.

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:57.560
So who would you be without the Great Omani?

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:59.240
Who would I be without the Great Omani?

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.200
A useless waste of...

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:10.520
I am still the oldest stuntman in the world.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:21.200
This is the restraint that the great Houdini once said is one of the hardest restraints in the world to get out of.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:23.040
And he knew what he was talking about.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:25.840
Right, now this boy really knows how to put the straitjacket on.

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:36.080
There you are, that's what not to do with your broken bottles.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:41.280
Well, Great Omani, you seem to have damaged your stomach a little.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:41.720
The stunt is dangerous.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:46.280
Obviously any stunts with fire or glass are dangerous.

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:53.360
That's why I said earlier, I don't know whether you caught this on camera, but I'd like to say I do hope nobody will try to reproduce these stunts in any way because they are, obviously, highly dangerous.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:54.160
Give him a kiss.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:54.840
Oh.

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:16.760
Oh.

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:17.960
Good girl.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:20.240
Oh, good.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:21.200
OK, was that all right?

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:27.240
Can I ask you how it's been recently, looking after your dad?

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:29.320
He seems to be improving.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:30.520
Yeah?

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:32.000
Oh, good.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:38.960
To me he seems a bit easier but trouble is, he's still planning stunts, which does my brain in.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:46.440
I can't, I can sit in the kitchen trying to watch television, "I've got a stunt, I might be able to do a stunt," and so I've got to shut the kitchen door.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:51.080
But just one thing, Dave, if I can do one last stunt, David.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:52.240
Yeah, but what can you do?

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.760
You can't even hold a glass, Dad.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:23:00.120
The only thing you can do is maybe stick a torch up your backside, alight.

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:02.360
Burn your prostate out.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:05.080
Cos in the old days...

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:06.320
All right, David.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.280
Burn it out.

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:09.000
All right.

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:14.960
I've got to, I've got to get one going somewhere.

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:21.400
Otherwise people will be saying - who is the Great Omani?

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:22.680
I don't want it to get to that stage.

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:32.720
Over the next few weeks, Ron's health deteriorated.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:37.000
Even so, he would not give up on the idea of performing again as the Great Omani.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:45.840
The last stunt has got to be the big one.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:45.960
Nothing less.

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:58.120
Ron needed looking after 24 hours a day and still didn't want to go into a care home.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:00.920
This was putting a lot of strain on David.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.680
So you wouldn't, you'd say no to Ron doing one little...

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:04.920
Yeah, yeah, I would, yeah.

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:07.560
I've had enough, honestly.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:14.280
He could do it if he went in a home, it would be a pleasure.

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:16.560
David.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:21.040
I'll cut your cigars out if you keep on.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:23.840
Bring me a cigar, Dave.

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.240
Come on, open.

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:41.800
Mmm.

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:45.160
You give me a call when you've had it, I'll put it out, OK?

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:54.000
What have you done?

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:02.400
Got it.

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:02.920
Have you?

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.080
No more.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.880
That was stupid.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:08.440
I'm going to sit down now and have a cup of tea.

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:09.600
You have a rest.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:10.280
You have a rest.

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:13.840
You may be the oldest stuntman in Britain, Dad, but I'm the oldest slave in Britain.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:16.040
Ha ha.

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:16.400
I like that.

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:18.280
I tell you what might be nice, Dave is...

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.840
Oh, what now, Dad!?

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:20.000
Oh.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:22.760
Sorry, sorry.

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:37.480
It must be a difficult time right now for him.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:41.360
Yeah and what about myself just sitting here seeing him crack up, see?

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:43.440
Quicker he goes, the better.

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:45.280
I'm not being cruel.

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:49.320
So I can, otherwise people say I'll end up dying.

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:52.160
Can't have that, can we Jessie?

00:26:07.480 --> 00:26:15.520
Within a fortnight, Ron had made another miraculous recovery and told me he was going to perform a stunt on Easter Sunday.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:19.880
Hello.

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:20.800
Come in, lad.

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:21.240
Come in.

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:21.440
Hello, Daniel.

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:21.840
Ron.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:22.120
Hello.

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:24.760
You on the zimmer already?

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.480
Yeah, I've just...

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:28.440
I get very lazy otherwise.

00:26:27.680 --> 00:26:31.000
Got the old camera and the sound...

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:31.880
You're looking very smart.

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:34.040
Thank you, thank you.

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:35.000
Yeah, very dapper.

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:36.400
How's your health since I last saw you, Ron?

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:41.400
You seem remarkably on form, considering last time I saw you you were...

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:45.600
Yeah, well, good days, bad days.

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:53.360
But, um... sometimes I feel like... funny thing is when the camera's on I always feel happy.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:58.080
But I have had some... well, you, you...

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:06.840
I've been an escapologist most of my life and a good one but this is one thing you can't escape from.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:10.800
You can beat man but you can't beat God and, er...

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:20.160
I got the illnesses so you've got to join them or you've got to adjust, adjust I think is possibly the word.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:23.440
Sometimes I'm fine and happy like I am today.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:25.040
Sometimes I get fits of depression.

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:27.280
But all in all I'm very lucky, I think.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:27.640
RRRR!

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:56.000
Once again, Ron had arranged a small media circus, promoting the event as the last ever performance of the Great Omani.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:28:04.960
He had also found a way round David's objections by getting the Mayor of Brighton to be his stage assistant.

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:12.160
I remember this young man 50 years ago when he used to jump off the West Pier into the water there, all chained up.

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:32.280
Ready?

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:33.800
Everyone ready?

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:34.040
Steady?

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:35.240
We all ready?

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:51.040
Thank you Mr Mayor, thank you very much.

00:28:54.680 --> 00:28:58.600
The weeks rolled by and I heard nothing from Ron.

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:01.600
But then I had a call from David.

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:03.720
Ron's health had taken another turn for the worse.

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:06.040
The cancer was spreading rapidly.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:10.440
He was almost completely paralysed and didn't have much longer to live.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:14.520
Hello, Daniel.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:17.200
I'll put his dinner away.

00:29:16.760 --> 00:29:18.480
Oh, that's all right.

00:29:17.560 --> 00:29:20.600
Let him eat Dave, let him eat.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:21.800
It's all right.

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:22.000
No, it's all right.

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:23.720
In the oven.

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:26.160
Hello, how are you doing?

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:26.680
Hello, Ron.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:29.560
Ah, you've got a new puppy.

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:40.960
David had borrowed the puppy from a friend in the hope it would cheer Ron up.

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:41.200
That's it, my old guard.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:42.600
Good boy.

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:03.680
He's got my comb.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:10.880
Jack, come and help, Jack.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:11.080
Jack.

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:11.800
Ah.

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:13.360
That was a sharp bite.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.720
No, Jack...

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:18.960
He's biting me.

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:20.720
Jack, no more.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:20.920
No.

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:21.280
Jack.

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:34.720
I think he can smell there's something wrong with him.

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:35.200
What?

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:35.680
Death.

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:38.120
Dogs can smell that.

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:39.080
Really?

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:41.920
Oh, didn't you know that?

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.640
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:59.920
Yeah oh, yeah.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:06.840
Doesn't seem quite himself.

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.560
He isn't, he isn't.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:14.160
Keeps breaking down crying all of the time now but... what do you expect?

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:14.560
Mmmm.

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:24.040
There's so little I understand in life.

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:31.520
I'm not quite sure what I'm doing...

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:35.080
I just find life a big mystery.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:47.880
I mean, for example, healthy strong male like myself, why should I be lying in a bed like this hardly able to move?

00:31:47.800 --> 00:31:52.480
That's a mystery why that should happen.

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:55.040
Well, you are 92.

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:57.520
You are 92.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:59.520
Part of the reason.

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:00.440
Yeah, that's...

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.960
That's old.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:07.520
That's ah, that's a reasonable answer.

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:11.920
And yet it came so suddenly.

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:21.560
Yes, I won't argue against that because I can't.

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:31.040
You miss the feeling... of confidence... and power.

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:33.280
It's just not there.

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:38.200
Why has it been taken away?

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:58.520
I could see that Ron had accepted that the Great Omani's powers were slipping away.

00:32:59.840 --> 00:33:08.080
But Ron still had one trick up his sleeve, an idea that would revive the Great Omani one last time.

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:14.000
Right, now we plan our next stunt.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:22.520
This is the Great Omani speaking.

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:25.840
Well, we, I think we've got quite a good story here.

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:35.200
Ron was planning his funeral and was calling all his contacts in the press to make sure the newspapers wouldn't forget to write his obituary.

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:38.520
This is Omani, the brightest stuntman, the Great Omani.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:41.840
There could be a story here.

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:46.480
The name's Omani, it should be fairly well known to you.

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:49.000
I may have a bit of a story for you.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:52.720
Yes, indeed, a very unusual story.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:56.400
You have heard of me, haven't you?

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.400
Oh, yes, of course you have.

00:33:57.560 --> 00:33:59.520
By the way...

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:00.800
No!

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:06.080
Jack.

00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:09.920
Once Ron had finished making his calls, he had a meeting with his undertaker.

00:34:12.679 --> 00:34:15.400
Please excuse us smoking.

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:15.840
Hello.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.560
Hello, I'm Theresa, hello.

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:19.080
Pleased to meet you.

00:34:18.199 --> 00:34:23.400
I'm very pleased to meet you, very honoured to meet you.

00:34:23.199 --> 00:34:25.639
Let's get the doggie off that way OK.

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:33.840
I'm not used to meeting ladies in your line of profession, as you may understand.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:34.440
But, um...

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:38.159
Are they all as pretty as you?

00:34:37.199 --> 00:34:38.719
Oh, I don't think so.

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:44.120
Ron went through his plans.

00:34:43.239 --> 00:34:51.520
He wanted white horses to pull his hearse through Brighton city centre and he'd written an epitaph to be read out at the end of the service.

00:34:50.679 --> 00:34:56.320
I go under the name of, of course, the Great Omani.

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:57.520
So here's my little poem.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:01.320
They put Omani in his box.

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:05.520
They're using nails instead of locks.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:10.720
But at his funeral, don't despair, The chances are he won't be there.

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:13.600
Do you like that one?

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:15.120
Yes, yes, it's good.

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:17.440
Thank you.

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:19.160
With that I'll say goodbye.

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:21.040
Thank you and God bless you.

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:21.640
All right.

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:22.720
You take care of yourself.

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:23.920
Nice to meet you.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:25.120
Lovely to meet you.

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:27.200
Thank you for coming, nice to meet you.

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:38.520
The Great Omani signing off... and thanking you very much indeed.

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:46.280
When I say the Great Omani I should say, what's left of him.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:49.520
We still salute you.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:53.560
Thank you and BBC, thank you very much for coming along.

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:07.160
A few days later, Ron passed away after a cigar and a glass of whisky.

00:36:06.320 --> 00:36:11.480
And as he had planned, his death did not go unnoticed.

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:36.960
I found his obituary featured in The Times, The Telegraph, The Independent, and dozens of papers in Britain and across the world, from the New Zealand Herald to the Illinois Journal.

00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:53.160
He's at peace.

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:59.360
Looks a lot younger and all.

00:36:58.520 --> 00:37:02.440
Actually, he never looked old anyhow for a 92, did he?

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:05.400
Has it been strange since he's gone?

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:10.800
The house is weird, I hear his voice but, then again, it's in my head at the moment.

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:12.040
What do you hear him saying?

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:17.960
Usual routine, five in the morning, "David, a cup of tea," or "David, my bag's full up."

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:22.160
Or if he's in a bad mood, "David, you lazy bastard."

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:27.920
You're peaceful now, Dad, aren't you, eh?

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:34.040
You'll be up with Houdini, all the doggies.

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:35.800
God rest you, Dad.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:36.080
I'm...

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:37.560
I'm afraid I have to go now.

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:40.400
Upsetting me a bit, which is understandable.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:41.600
He's at peace.
